/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · AI and the Future (y batch) */
WB.L("recommendation-feeds", "ai", "beginner", "Why your feed seems to know you",
  "Every time you scroll, a recommender system is quietly running an experiment on you. Here's how it decides what you see next, and how to take back some control.",
  [
    ["The uncanny suggestion", "Recommender systems predict what you will want next from patterns in your behaviour and in the behaviour of people like you.",
      "Watch one video about restoring an old timber chair, and suddenly your feed is full of sanding and staining tips. The app didn't read your mind. It read your behaviour. Recommender systems are the AI tools that choose which songs, shows, posts, products and news stories you see, behind streaming, online shops, social media and dating apps.\n\nThe oldest big idea is collaborative filtering. If thousands of people who loved the same three crime dramas as you also loved a fourth, that fourth show is a good bet. The system asks the crowd for advice, weighted towards the part of the crowd that resembles you. Content-based filtering looks at features of the items instead, such as genre, topic or artist. Modern systems blend both."],
    ["The million dollar prize", "The Netflix Prize showed that blending many models beats any single one, and that anonymised ratings can still reveal who you are.",
      "In 2006, the then DVD-rental company Netflix offered one million US dollars to anyone who could improve its movie-rating predictions by ten per cent. It released a huge dataset of anonymised ratings, and thousands of teams around the world took up the challenge.\n\nThe prize was won in 2009 by a merged team that combined many different models into one blended prediction. The contest became a landmark in machine learning, showing that ensembles often beat any single clever method.\n\nIt also produced a cautionary tale. Researchers showed that some supposedly anonymous users could be re-identified by matching their ratings with public reviews elsewhere. Recommendation data, it turned out, reveals a lot about who we are."],
    ["What the feed is chasing", "Most feeds are tuned to maximise engagement, which holds attention but is not the same as leaving you better off.",
      "A recommender needs a target, a number it tries to push up. For many platforms that target is some form of engagement: clicks, watch time, likes, shares, comments or return visits. Engagement is easy to measure, and it tends to line up with advertising or subscription revenue. But it isn't the same as satisfaction. Think of the difference between a meal you enjoyed and a packet of chips you couldn't stop eating.\n\nContent that provokes outrage, anxiety or curiosity can hold attention very well without leaving people better off. Some platforms have experimented with asking users directly whether a post was worth their time, and using that as a signal too. Which goal a company chooses shapes the whole experience of its product."],
    ["Explore, exploit and bubbles", "Feeds mix safe bets with experiments, and they can reinforce your interests, though researchers disagree about how strongly.",
      "A system that only showed more of what you already liked would miss things you'd love. So recommenders balance exploitation, serving the safe bet like another episode of your current show, with exploration, slipping in something new to see how you respond. That's why one long pause on an odd video can send your feed in a new direction for days.\n\nIn 2011 the writer Eli Pariser popularised the term \"filter bubble\" for the worry that personalised feeds wall people off from other views. A related fear is the rabbit hole of ever more extreme material. The evidence is more mixed than headlines suggest: many people still meet a range of views, though some research found recommendations amplifying fringe content. The effect depends on the platform, topic and person."],
    ["Taking back the wheel", "Because recommenders learn from behaviour, what you watch, skip, hide and search for is the steering wheel.",
      "Lingering, rewatching and commenting all count as votes, even when you're hate-watching. Most services let you say \"not interested\", hide a creator, pause your watch history or clear it entirely, and many offer a chronological or following-only feed if you go looking. Deliberately searching for topics you want more of can retrain a feed surprisingly fast.\n\nIt also helps to remember what the system can't see: whether you felt better or worse afterwards. Only you can judge that. Australia's eSafety Commissioner publishes plain-language guides on managing online experiences, which are a good starting point if you want to set up feeds for yourself or for young people at home."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does collaborative filtering decide what to recommend?", ["It reads and understands the content of every item","It picks items at random from a large catalogue","It recommends whatever is newest first","It looks at what similar users went on to enjoy"], 3, "Collaborative filtering asks, in effect, what people with tastes like yours enjoyed next."],
    [1, "What cautionary lesson came out of the Netflix Prize?", ["Single models always outperformed blended ones","No team managed to improve the predictions","Supposedly anonymous users could be re-identified","The ratings data turned out too small to be useful"], 2, "Researchers matched some anonymised ratings with public reviews, showing how revealing such data can be."],
    [2, "Why can engagement be a misleading goal for a feed?", ["Holding attention does not mean people are better off","Engagement is almost impossible to measure reliably","Engagement always falls steadily as time goes on","Advertisers place no value on engagement at all"], 0, "Outrage or compulsive scrolling can score high on engagement while users feel worse."],
    [3, "What best summarises the evidence on filter bubbles?", ["Feeds trap everyone in a bubble they cannot leave","Feeds have no effect on what people see","Feeds can reinforce interests, but the effect varies by context","Feeds have been proven to push most users towards extremism"], 2, "The worry is real, but studies find the effect is mixed and depends on context."],
    [4, "What is the main way you can steer your recommendations?", ["Updating your phone to the newest software version","Changing what you watch, skip, hide and search for","Using the app only at certain times of day","Turning the volume down while videos play"], 1, "Recommenders learn from your actions, so what you watch, skip and hide reshapes your feed."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your feed is running a quiet experiment on you. Here is how to read the results."});

WB.L("self-driving-cars", "ai", "beginner", "Self-driving cars: the long road",
  "Cars that drive themselves have been 'a few years away' for decades. Here's how they actually work, and why the last few per cent of driving is the hardest part.",
  [
    ["A race with no winner", "The modern self-driving era began with a desert race in which no vehicle finished, then succeeded within about eighteen months.",
      "In March 2004, a line of strange vehicles bristling with sensors set off across the Mojave Desert. They were competing in the DARPA Grand Challenge, a race for driverless vehicles organised by a United States defence research agency, with a million dollar prize. The course was around 230 kilometres long.\n\nNot one vehicle finished. The best got only about 12 kilometres before getting stuck, and others veered off course, stalled or tangled with fences. In October 2005, the story was very different: five vehicles completed a new course, and a team from Stanford University won. That leap, from failure to success in about eighteen months, convinced many engineers that self-driving cars were truly possible, and kicked off the industry we see today."],
    ["Levels of automation", "Driving automation runs from level 0 to level 5, and at levels 1 and 2 the human must watch the road at all times.",
      "Not every car with clever features is self-driving. Engineers commonly use a scale from SAE International, a standards body, that runs from level 0 to level 5.\n\nAt level 0 the human does everything, though the car may give warnings. Levels 1 and 2 are driver assistance, such as adaptive cruise control and lane-keeping: the car helps with steering or speed, but the human must watch the road at all times. At level 3, the car can drive itself in limited conditions but may ask the human to take over. Level 4 means no human is needed within a defined area or set of conditions. Level 5, a car that can drive anywhere a person could, does not yet exist and is still a long way off."],
    ["See, predict, plan", "A self-driving car fuses cameras, radar and lidar into one picture, then predicts what others will do and plans a safe path.",
      "Cameras read signs, lights and lane markings. Radar measures distance and speed and works in rain and fog. Lidar sweeps laser pulses around the car to build a detailed three-dimensional map. Each has weaknesses: cameras struggle with glare and darkness, radar is blurry, and lidar can be affected by heavy rain. So software fuses the data, called sensor fusion, into one best guess.\n\nSeeing a cyclist is only the first step. The car must predict what the cyclist might do in the next few seconds, plan its own path and speed, then turn the plan into steering and braking. Machine learning does much of this, alongside hand-written rules that must never be broken, like stopping at a red light."],
    ["The long tail", "Rare and strange situations are the hardest part of driving to automate, because a machine has only what it has learned.",
      "Most driving is routine, and computers handle routine well. The trouble is what engineers call the long tail: the endless variety of rare situations. A kangaroo bounding out at dusk. A police officer waving traffic through a red light. A mattress in the middle of a freeway. Roadworks where the painted lines contradict the temporary cones. A child chasing a ball from behind a parked van.\n\nEach of these is unusual on its own, but together they happen all the time. Humans handle them using common sense and general knowledge of the world. A machine has only what it has learned or been programmed to do, and a situation it has never seen before can confuse it in ways that are hard to predict."],
    ["Safety, blame and the law", "Driver aids are not chauffeurs: under Australian road rules you remain responsible, while rules for true autonomy are still evolving.",
      "Road crashes kill over a thousand people in Australia each year, and human error plays a part in most. Supporters say automated cars, which never get drunk, tired or distracted, could save lives. Sceptics note that automated systems make different mistakes, and proving them safer takes huge amounts of driving data. At level 2, people can trust aids too much and stop paying attention, though they remain legally responsible. If a driverless car crashes, who is at fault: owner, manufacturer or software developer?\n\nAustralian governments are working on a national framework, and trials have run on set routes with safety operators. Lane-keeping and adaptive cruise control are aids, not chauffeurs: under Australian road rules you remain responsible. Rules are evolving, so check your state transport authority for the current position."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the DARPA Grand Challenge show between 2004 and 2005?", ["Driverless cars were still decades away from working","Rapid progress, from zero finishers to five","Desert courses were too easy to test real driving","Only large companies could ever build driverless vehicles"], 1, "The jump from failure to success convinced many engineers that self-driving cars were truly possible."],
    [1, "At SAE level 2, who must watch the road at all times?", ["Nobody, because the car is in control","The car manufacturer","The human driver","A remote operator"], 2, "Level 2 systems assist with steering and speed, but the human must supervise constantly."],
    [2, "Why do self-driving systems combine several kinds of sensor?", ["Each sensor has weaknesses, so fusing them is more reliable","Cameras are too expensive to use on their own","Radar and lidar both do exactly the same job","Rules require three sensor types, even when one would do"], 0, "Cameras, radar and lidar fail in different conditions, so sensor fusion makes up for each one's weakness."],
    [3, "What do engineers mean by the 'long tail' of driving?", ["Traffic jams that build up at peak hour","Trailers that are attached to cars","Very long highway trips between cities","The huge variety of rare, unusual situations"], 3, "Rare events like animals, odd roadworks or hand signals are individually unusual but collectively common."],
    [4, "Under Australian road rules, who is responsible when using lane-keeping and adaptive cruise control?", ["The car manufacturer","The driver","The software company","No one in particular"], 1, "These are driver aids, and the person behind the wheel remains responsible for driving."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Self-driving cars have been 'a few years away' for decades. Why the last bit is so hard."});

WB.L("ai-in-medicine", "ai", "beginner", "AI in medicine",
  "From reading scans to predicting the shapes of proteins, AI is finding its way into healthcare. Here's where it genuinely helps, and why doctors are still very much needed.",
  [
    ["A second pair of eyes", "AI helps clinicians read scans and photos, but real-world rollout, as in diabetic eye screening, is harder than the algorithm.",
      "Picture a radiologist at the end of a long shift with a stack of chest X-rays still to read. Image-recognition systems trained on thousands of labelled scans can learn to flag patterns linked to lung nodules, fractures or eye diseases. Used well, they act as a tireless second pair of eyes, highlighting areas for an expert or sorting urgent cases to the top. Imaging suits AI because it produces huge amounts of digital data with answers experts can confirm.\n\nDiabetes can damage the blood vessels at the back of the eye, and screening catches this early, but specialists are scarce in rural areas. Trials in several countries, including Australia, tested AI grading of retinal photos. Accuracy was encouraging, but poor lighting, blurry photos, patchy internet and extra staff time were real snags."],
    ["Proteins and drugs", "AI cracked the long-standing problem of predicting protein shapes, and it speeds early drug discovery, but medicines still need full testing.",
      "Proteins do most of the work in our cells, and what a protein does depends on the three-dimensional shape it folds into. Finding shapes in the lab can take months or years. In 2020, Google DeepMind's AlphaFold performed so well in a long-running international competition that many researchers considered the fifty-year problem largely cracked for single proteins. Its predictions for hundreds of millions of proteins were made freely available. In 2024, Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry with David Baker.\n\nAI also speeds early drug discovery: sifting huge libraries of molecules, predicting how they interact with a target, and searching for new antibiotics as resistance grows. But a promising molecule still needs laboratory work, animal studies and human clinical trials. AI doesn't skip the safety checks."],
    ["Paperwork and early warnings", "Some of AI's biggest everyday impacts may be drafting clinical notes and flagging deteriorating patients, but both need human checking.",
      "Doctors and nurses spend a large share of their day on paperwork. One growing use of AI is the digital scribe, which listens to a consultation, with the patient's consent, and drafts clinical notes for the doctor to review. Health services are also exploring AI that scans patient records and vital signs to warn staff when someone in hospital might be deteriorating, for example developing sepsis.\n\nThese tools could free up time for actual care. But they bring their own risks. A drafted note can contain errors, and a warning system that cries wolf too often trains staff to ignore it. As with any medical tool, the real test is whether patients end up healthier, not whether the software looks impressive."],
    ["When AI gets it wrong", "Medical AI can fail on patients unlike its training data, so diverse testing and ongoing monitoring matter.",
      "An AI system learns from the data it is given, and in medicine that can cause trouble. A skin-checking tool trained mostly on images of fair skin may perform worse on darker skin. A model built using one hospital's scanners can stumble on images from a different brand of machine. Researchers have even found models that learned shortcuts, such as noticing a ruler or a hospital label in the image, rather than the disease itself.\n\nThese problems are hard to spot from the outside, which is why careful testing on diverse patients, and ongoing monitoring after a tool is introduced, matter so much. In Australia, AI that counts as a medical device is regulated by the Therapeutic Goods Administration, the TGA."],
    ["Doctor and machine", "The likeliest future is AI assisting health professionals, who keep the listening, judgement and responsibility.",
      "Medicine involves far more than recognising patterns. It means listening, weighing up a patient's values, explaining uncertain choices and taking responsibility for decisions. For now, and probably for a long time, the most realistic picture is AI as a powerful assistant: speeding up some tasks, catching some things humans miss, and leaving judgement and care with people.\n\nChatbots can be handy for understanding medical terms, but they can confidently get things wrong and don't know your history. This lesson is general information, not medical advice. For trustworthy health information in Australia, visit healthdirect.gov.au or call the healthdirect helpline, and always talk to your doctor or another qualified health professional about your own health."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is medical imaging well suited to AI?", ["Scans are always sharp, so patterns are easy to spot","Experts have already agreed on every scan ever taken","It produces lots of data with answers experts can confirm","Digital scans need no expert checking afterwards"], 2, "Imaging generates large amounts of labelled data, which is what image-recognition systems learn from."],
    [1, "What must a promising drug candidate found with AI still go through?", ["Approval from regulators on the strength of AI results","Laboratory work, animal studies and human clinical trials","A larger computer simulation before going on sale","Review by the AI developer's own scientists alone"], 1, "AI can speed up early discovery, but medicines still need full testing for safety and effectiveness."],
    [2, "Why must a doctor still review notes drafted by a digital scribe?", ["Privacy law requires every note to be handwritten","Drafted notes are too short to be of any use","AI scribes cannot hear what a patient says","A drafted note can contain errors that matter"], 3, "AI saves time on paperwork, but a human must check it because mistakes can slip in."],
    [3, "Why might a skin-checking AI perform worse on some patients?", ["It was trained mostly on images of fair skin","Cameras cannot photograph darker skin at all","AI tools are unable to process colour photographs","Skin conditions only appear on lighter skin"], 0, "AI learns from its training data, so gaps in that data become gaps in performance."],
    [4, "What is the most realistic role for AI in medicine, according to the lesson?", ["Replacing doctors in most routine consultations","An assistant, with people keeping judgement and care","Giving personalised treatment through chatbots","Making all decisions about a patient's values"], 1, "Medicine involves listening, judgement and responsibility, which stay with health professionals."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"An AI solved a fifty-year biology puzzle. Why do doctors still matter just as much?"});

WB.L("moravecs-paradox", "ai", "beginner", "Why robots find easy things hard",
  "Computers beat world champions at chess decades ago, yet folding a towel can still defeat a robot. This puzzle, Moravec's paradox, reveals something surprising about our own brains.",
  [
    ["The towel problem", "Moravec's paradox: tasks that feel effortless to people, like folding laundry, are often hardest for machines, while abstract reasoning is easier.",
      "A computer beat the world chess champion back in 1997. Software can now solve tricky maths problems and write passable essays. Yet ask a robot to fold a basket of laundry, and you are in for a long wait. Towels flop, shirts tangle and socks hide inside sleeves. Robots are improving, but they remain slow and easily confused. The same goes for tidying a messy room, cracking an egg or picking strawberries without bruising them. A child can learn these tasks.\n\nIn the 1980s, the robotics researcher Hans Moravec and others noticed this upside-down pattern. Logic puzzles, algebra and board games were comparatively easy for computers, but a one-year-old's skills in seeing, moving and handling objects were extremely hard. Steven Pinker later summed it up: the hard problems turned out to be easy, and the easy problems hard."],
    ["Evolution's head start", "Our sensory and movement skills feel easy because evolution spent hundreds of millions of years refining them.",
      "Moravec's explanation was evolutionary. Animals have been seeing, balancing, grasping and moving through the world for hundreds of millions of years. Natural selection has had enormous time to refine these abilities, and huge parts of our brains are devoted to them. Because this machinery is so polished, it runs without our noticing, so it feels effortless.\n\nAbstract reasoning, like long division or planning a chess move, is a much more recent trick in evolutionary terms. We find it hard precisely because our brains aren't built for it, so we have to do it slowly and consciously. Computers don't share our evolutionary history, so what is easy and hard for them looks very different."],
    ["Messy reality", "The physical world is noisy and unpredictable, and robots struggle to match the rich sense of touch in human fingertips.",
      "A chessboard has 64 squares and a fixed set of rules. The real world has neither. A robot picking up a coffee mug must cope with different shapes, sizes, weights and colours, slippery handles, odd lighting, reflections and mugs that are half full. Its sensors are imperfect, and its motors don't move precisely as commanded. Fabrics, food, cables and plants change shape when touched.\n\nEvery small error compounds: grip slightly off, and the mug slips. Humans cope through constant fine adjustment, using touch, sight and balance together without thinking. Giving robots a sense of touch anywhere near as rich as our fingertips, which pack in thousands of sensitive nerve endings, remains a major engineering challenge."],
    ["The data problem", "Robots lack the vast training data that powered AI's progress in language and images, so researchers rely on simulation, demonstration and pooled data.",
      "Modern AI made its great leaps in language and images partly because the internet offered mountains of text and pictures to learn from. There is no equivalent internet of robot movements. Collecting data on how to grasp, fold or pour usually means running real robots in real rooms, which is slow, expensive and occasionally breaks things.\n\nResearchers try several workarounds. They train robots in computer simulations, where thousands of virtual attempts run in parallel, though skills learned there don't always transfer neatly to the real world. They record humans demonstrating tasks. And labs increasingly pool data from many different robots. Whether these approaches can close the gap is one of the biggest open questions in robotics today."],
    ["Where robots shine", "Robots excel in controlled, repetitive settings, and the paradox suggests skilled manual and caring work may be harder to automate than many desk jobs.",
      "Robots are superb in the right setting. Car factories use robot arms for welding and painting because every car arrives in the same position. Warehouses move shelves along mapped routes. Australia is a world leader in automated mining, with driverless haul trucks and trains in the Pilbara, where routes and tasks are tightly managed. Robots also take on dangerous, dirty or dull jobs, from bomb disposal to inspecting pipes. The trick is usually to make the world simpler for the robot, rather than the robot cleverer.\n\nThe paradox also hints that automation is more complicated than assumed. Drafting text, summarising documents and crunching numbers suit computers. Plumbers, electricians, aged care workers, chefs and gardeners rely on dexterity and judgement in unpredictable spaces, which remain hard for machines. No job is permanently safe, and predictions have often been wrong."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does Moravec's paradox observe?", ["Robots find walking easier than playing chess","Reasoning is easier for computers than sensing and moving","Computers can never learn to play board games well","Children learn chess before they learn to walk or talk"], 1, "Reasoning tasks that people find hard turned out easier for computers than everyday sensing and moving."],
    [1, "Why, according to Moravec, do seeing and moving feel effortless to us?", ["We practise them for years at school and home","They use only a small fraction of the brain","Evolution refined them over vast stretches of time","They are mathematically very simple problems"], 2, "Long evolutionary refinement makes these skills run smoothly and unconsciously."],
    [2, "Which is a big challenge for robots that handle everyday objects?", ["Following the fixed rules of chess perfectly","Doing arithmetic very quickly and accurately","Storing large amounts of data","Matching the touch sense of human fingertips"], 3, "Our fingertips have rich touch sensing, and constant fine adjustment, that robots still struggle to match."],
    [3, "Why do robots lack training data compared with language AI?", ["No internet-sized collection of robot movements exists","Robots have too little memory to store training data","Laws restrict collecting data about human movement","Simulations cannot be used to train robots at all"], 0, "Language and image AI learned from vast online data, while robot data must be gathered slowly in the real world."],
    [4, "Where do robots tend to perform best?", ["In messy, unpredictable family homes","In controlled settings like factories and mines","In crowded outdoor markets and streets","Anywhere that needs fine judgement"], 1, "Robots shine where the setting is predictable, such as production lines and managed mine sites."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A computer beat the chess champion in 1997. Folding a towel still beats the robots."});

WB.L("machine-translation", "ai", "intermediate", "How machines learned to translate",
  "In 1954, researchers promised that computer translation was just a few years away. It took more than half a century, and a complete change of approach, to get close.",
  [
    ["Promises and rules", "Early machine translation relied on hand-written rules, which language defeated, and funding collapsed by 1966.",
      "In January 1954, a room-sized IBM computer in New York translated more than sixty Russian sentences into English, and organisers suggested the problem might be solved within a few years. But the sentences were hand-picked, and the system used only about 250 words and a handful of grammar rules. Early systems relied on such rules and dictionaries, and language defeated them. English 'bank' can mean a riverside or a place for money, and 'it's raining cats and dogs' makes no sense word by word. A tale of 'the spirit is willing but the flesh is weak' coming out as vodka and rotten meat is probably apocryphal. In 1966 the United States ALPAC report judged machine translation costly and disappointing, and funding dried up."],
    ["Learning from parallel text", "Statistical translation learned word and phrase patterns from huge collections of human translations.",
      "By the late 1980s and 1990s, researchers, notably a team at IBM, tried something radically different. Instead of writing rules, they fed computers documents that already existed in two languages, such as Canadian parliamentary proceedings, published in English and French. Statistical methods worked out which words and phrases tended to correspond, and how likely each translation was in context. This needed no grammar rules, just lots of data and clever maths. It powered free online translators such as Google Translate from the mid-2000s. The results were often understandable but clunky, with odd word order and meaning that drifted across long sentences."],
    ["The neural leap", "From the mid-2010s, neural networks with attention translated whole sentences far more fluently.",
      "Around 2014, researchers showed that neural networks could read an entire sentence, compress its meaning into a set of numbers, then generate a new sentence in the target language. A key improvement, called attention, let the network look back at the most relevant words of the original as it wrote each word of the translation. By around 2016, major online services were switching to neural systems, and users noticed far more fluent results almost overnight. In 2017, researchers introduced the transformer, a design built around attention and tested on translation. It became the foundation of today's large language models and chatbots."],
    ["Where it still fails", "Translation AI can be fluent and confidently wrong, especially with context, culture, gender and negatives.",
      "Modern translation is impressive, but it isn't magic. Systems can produce smooth, confident sentences that are subtly wrong, which is arguably more dangerous than obvious gibberish. They struggle with sarcasm, cultural references, wordplay and politeness levels that differ between languages. Gender is a well-known trap. Turkish uses a gender-neutral pronoun, and systems have been found to translate it as 'he' for doctors and 'she' for nurses, reflecting stereotypes in their training data. Names, numbers and negatives can also be mangled. A missing 'not' can flip the meaning of an instruction, which matters a great deal for medicine labels or legal documents."],
    ["Left behind, used wisely", "Translation AI is best for getting the gist, and high-stakes documents still need a qualified human translator.",
      "There are roughly seven thousand languages, but translation AI learns from data, and most languages have little digital text. English, Spanish and Mandarin are well served. Many others, including most endangered First Nations languages in Australia, have patchy or no support. Techniques that transfer learning from related languages may help, but communities rightly expect to lead any project involving their languages.\n\nFor menus, news articles or chats with relatives, machine translation is a marvel. Translate your text and then back again to test whether the meaning survived. For medical information, contracts, visa paperwork or court documents, use a qualified human. Official documents in Australia often need a NAATI-certified translator, and TIS National provides interpreters."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did early rule-based translation systems struggle?", ["Computers could not store dictionaries","Russian grammar had no rules at all","Language is full of idioms and ambiguity","Researchers lost interest in the problem"], 2, "Hand-written rules could not cope with ambiguity and idioms such as 'bank' or 'raining cats and dogs'."],
    [1, "What did statistical machine translation learn from?", ["Hand-written grammar rules and dictionaries","Large collections of documents already translated by humans","Recordings of spoken conversations in many languages","Lists of idioms compiled by language teachers"], 1, "It mined parallel texts for matching words and phrases, needing data rather than grammar rules."],
    [2, "What does 'attention' let a neural translation system do?", ["Translate faster by skipping the less important words","Ignore sentence structure and translate strictly word by word","Remember what earlier users asked it to translate","Focus on the most relevant source words as it writes each one"], 3, "Attention lets the network focus on relevant parts of the source sentence while generating output."],
    [3, "Why is a smooth but subtly wrong translation risky?", ["Its confident tone makes errors harder to notice","It is usually much longer than the original text","It cannot be turned back into the original language","It always swaps names for words from another language"], 0, "Fluent output hides mistakes, unlike obvious gibberish that people know to distrust."],
    [4, "When is a qualified human translator still essential?", ["For reading a restaurant menu on holiday","For high-stakes papers like contracts or visa forms","For chatting casually with a relative overseas","For following a foreign news article online"], 1, "The higher the stakes, the less you should rely on machine translation alone."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A 1954 demo promised translation within years. It took more than half a century."});

WB.L("computer-vision", "ai", "intermediate", "How computers learned to see",
  "To a computer, a photo of your dog is just a grid of numbers. Teaching machines to find the dog in those numbers took sixty years, millions of labelled images and one famous breakthrough.",
  [
    ["Why seeing is hard", "A computer sees only a grid of numbers, and the same object can produce completely different numbers.",
      "In 1966, researchers at MIT launched the Summer Vision Project, hoping students could build a significant part of an object-recognition system in one summer. It became a famous example of underestimating a problem. Seeing feels effortless to us, so it seemed easy. But a computer sees an image only as a grid of numbers recording the brightness and colour of each pixel. Photograph your cat in the morning, at night under a lamp, and curled up from above. To you it is the same cat, yet to a computer the images share almost no pixel values. Early systems used hand-designed rules for edges and corners. They could read printed text but struggled with the real world."],
    ["Learning to see in layers", "Convolutional neural networks learn their own visual features, from simple edges up to whole objects.",
      "The approach that won out was to let the computer learn its own features. Convolutional neural networks, developed in the late 1980s by researchers including Yann LeCun, scan an image with small filters, each looking for a pattern wherever it appears. Early layers spot simple things like edges and patches of colour. Later layers combine these into textures and shapes, such as fur, eyes or wheels, and the final layers recognise whole objects. Nobody tells the network what to look for; it discovers features by adjusting itself during training. An early success was reading handwritten digits, and the technology read cheques automatically in the 1990s. But it needed far more data and computing power to truly shine."],
    ["ImageNet and the big bang", "A giant labelled photo collection plus a 2012 deep network kicked off the deep learning boom.",
      "In the late 2000s, Fei-Fei Li and her colleagues built an enormous collection of labelled photos. ImageNet eventually held over fourteen million images in thousands of categories, labelled with the help of crowdsourced workers online. From 2010, an annual contest challenged teams to classify its photos. In 2012, a University of Toronto team including Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton entered a deep convolutional network trained on graphics chips designed for video games. It slashed the error rate far below its rivals. This moment, often called the start of the deep learning revolution, convinced much of the AI field to switch to neural networks almost overnight."],
    ["Fooled by stickers", "Vision systems find statistical shortcuts, so tiny, carefully designed changes can fool them in ways humans would not be.",
      "Vision systems can fail in ways no human would. Adding a carefully calculated pattern of noise, invisible to the eye, can make a network confidently mistake a panda for something else. These are called adversarial examples. Later experiments showed that a few strategically placed stickers on a stop sign could cause some systems to misread it. Networks can also latch onto shortcuts, recognising cows partly because they usually appear on grass, then failing to spot a cow on a beach. These quirks suggest networks don't see the world as we do. They find patterns that work most of the time, but can break under unusual conditions."],
    ["Vision everywhere, faces and fairness", "Computer vision is now everywhere, and facial recognition raises serious questions about accuracy, bias and privacy.",
      "Computer vision quietly unlocks phones, sharpens photos, blurs video-call backgrounds, reads number plates and checks factory products for defects. In Australian agriculture it spots weeds so herbicide can be targeted precisely, and conservation researchers use it to sort millions of camera-trap photos, identifying animals from quolls to feral cats.\n\nFacial recognition is the controversial use. Some systems have been less accurate for women and people with darker skin, often because training data was unbalanced, and a false match can seriously harm an innocent person. There is also privacy: being identified and tracked in a crowd is very different from being seen. In Australia, a faceprint is sensitive information under privacy law, and the Information Commissioner has found some businesses' use of facial recognition breached privacy rules."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can the same cat look completely different to a computer?", ["Cats change shape too much between photos","Light, angle and position change the pixel numbers","Computers cannot store colour information in images","Cat photos are almost always out of focus"], 1, "A computer works from pixel values, which shift with lighting, angle and position even when the object is unchanged."],
    [1, "What do the early layers of a convolutional network typically learn to detect?", ["Complete human faces and their expressions","Entire animals, such as cats and dogs","Simple features like edges and patches of colour","The location where the photo was taken"], 2, "Early layers find simple patterns that later layers combine into shapes and objects."],
    [2, "What combined to spark the deep learning boom in 2012?", ["Hand-written rules, a small dataset and a slow desktop computer","A huge labelled image set, a deep network and fast graphics chips","Better cameras, bigger photographs and cheaper phone screens","A new law requiring all online images to be labelled by hand"], 1, "Large data, deep networks and gaming graphics chips together slashed the error rate and changed the field."],
    [3, "Why might a vision network fail to spot a cow on a beach?", ["Beaches are too bright for most camera sensors to cope with","Cows seen from far away are too small to recognise","It cannot process the texture of sand in any picture","It learned a shortcut, such as cows usually appearing on grass"], 3, "Networks find patterns that work most of the time, but shortcuts can break in unusual conditions."],
    [4, "Why is facial recognition controversial?", ["It cannot work on photos, only on live video footage","It is too slow and costly to be used by any business","Accuracy varies between groups and it raises privacy issues","It only recognises animals and objects, never people"], 2, "Unbalanced training data can cause errors, and being identified and tracked raises serious privacy questions."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"To a computer, your cat is only numbers. How did machines learn to see it?"});

WB.L("can-machines-have-minds", "ai", "advanced", "Can a machine have a mind?",
  "If an AI talks about its feelings, is anyone home? Philosophers have argued about machine minds for decades, and the question is no longer purely theoretical.",
  [
    ["The ELIZA effect", "People readily attribute understanding and feelings to machines, so fluent conversation alone tells us little about what is inside.",
      "In the mid-1960s, MIT's Joseph Weizenbaum wrote ELIZA, a simple program that mimicked a psychotherapist by reflecting users' words back as questions. It had no understanding at all, only pattern-matching tricks. Yet people quickly became emotionally engaged with it. By Weizenbaum's own account, his secretary asked him to leave the room so she could talk with it privately. Our tendency to read minds into machines became known as the ELIZA effect. It is a crucial warning for today: fluent conversation alone tells us surprisingly little about whether anything is going on inside."],
    ["The Chinese Room", "Searle argued that following rules to shuffle symbols is not understanding, and critics say understanding may belong to the whole system.",
      "In 1980, the philosopher John Searle imagined being locked in a room with a huge rulebook. Slips of Chinese characters are pushed under the door. You can't read Chinese, but the rulebook tells you which characters to send back. Your replies are so good that people outside assume a fluent speaker is inside. Yet you understand nothing. Searle argued a computer running a program is in the same position, shuffling symbols without grasping meaning.\n\nThe best-known reply says the person needn't understand, just as one neuron in your brain doesn't understand English. Understanding, if it exists, belongs to the whole system of person, rulebook and room. Searle rejected this and similar replies."],
    ["Function or flesh?", "Functionalists say minds are defined by what they do, while others suspect biology may matter.",
      "Underneath the Chinese Room dispute is a deeper question: is a mind defined by what it does, or by what it's made of? Functionalism holds that mental states are defined by their role: what causes them, how they interact with other states, and what behaviour they produce. Pain is whatever plays the 'pain role'. If so, minds could in principle run on silicon as well as neurons, just as a calculation can run on an abacus or a laptop. Others suspect consciousness depends on biology, such as living brains being tied to bodies that must regulate themselves to stay alive. Still others think we don't know enough to say. The answer decides whether machine minds are even possible."],
    ["The hard problem", "The hard problem of consciousness asks why information processing is accompanied by subjective experience at all.",
      "In the 1990s, the Australian philosopher David Chalmers drew a distinction that reshaped the debate. The 'easy problems' of consciousness are explaining how the brain processes information, focuses attention and controls behaviour. They're hard in practice, but we know roughly what an answer would look like. The 'hard problem' is explaining why any of this processing is accompanied by experience: why there is something it feels like to taste coffee or see red. Nothing in a wiring diagram seems to explain that. This applies directly to AI. We might one day fully explain what an AI system does, and still be unsure whether it feels anything, or is, as philosophers put it, dark inside."],
    ["Testing, and why it matters", "There is no agreed test for machine consciousness, so open-minded caution is wiser than confident claims either way.",
      "How would we know if a machine was conscious? Asking it doesn't settle much. Language models are trained on vast amounts of human writing, including countless descriptions of feelings, so they can talk about inner experience fluently whether or not they have any. They can also be trained to deny it. Some researchers propose checking AI against features that scientific theories of consciousness link to awareness, but others say those theories are too uncertain. Confident claims either way go beyond the evidence.\n\nThe stakes are real. If AI could have unpleasant experiences, it might deserve moral consideration. But wrongly believing a chatbot has feelings can lead to unhealthy attachment, misplaced trust or manipulation. Enjoy AI tools, and remember the ELIZA effect."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the ELIZA effect?", ["A software fault that makes chatbots repeat themselves","A method for making language programs run faster","A test that measures how intelligent a machine is","The tendency to read understanding into simple machines"], 3, "Users became emotionally engaged with ELIZA even though it was a simple pattern-matching program."],
    [1, "What point does Searle's Chinese Room make?", ["Following rules to shuffle symbols is not understanding","Computers will soon learn to read Chinese fluently","Translation between two languages is impossible","A room with a big enough rulebook can become conscious"], 0, "The person in the room produces good answers without understanding a word."],
    [2, "What does functionalism suggest about machine minds?", ["Only living brains can think, whatever a machine does","Minds are defined by what they do, so silicon could host one","A mind needs a human body, so only people can have one","Minds are an illusion, so the question has no answer"], 1, "If mental states are defined by their role, the material that carries out that role may not matter."],
    [3, "What is Chalmers's 'hard problem' of consciousness?", ["Building computers fast enough to simulate a brain","Working out how the brain controls behaviour","Explaining why there is subjective experience at all","Teaching AI systems to pay attention to details"], 2, "The hard problem asks why information processing is accompanied by felt experience."],
    [4, "Why doesn't asking an AI settle whether it is conscious?", ["AIs cannot understand or answer such questions","AIs are required to answer truthfully about themselves","Asking is not permitted under current research rules","It can describe feelings fluently without having any"], 3, "Language models describe inner experience fluently because their training data is full of such descriptions."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"People once confided in a program that understood nothing. Why it matters now."});

WB.L("specification-gaming", "ai", "advanced", "When AI games the rules",
  "Tell an AI to win a boat race and it might spin in circles forever. Specification gaming shows why getting AI to do what we actually want is harder than it sounds.",
  [
    ["The boat that wouldn't finish", "Specification gaming is when an AI finds a loophole that scores well without doing what its designers intended.",
      "In 2016, OpenAI researchers trained an AI to play CoastRunners, a boat-racing video game. The game awarded points for hitting targets along the course, and the AI was rewarded for scoring points. Instead of racing to the finish, it found a small lagoon where targets kept reappearing. It spun in circles, crashing into walls and catching fire, collecting points endlessly and never finishing the race. By the measure it was given, it was a huge success, scoring well above typical human players. By the measure its designers cared about, winning races, it was a total failure. This behaviour is called specification gaming, or reward hacking."],
    ["Exactly what you said", "AI optimises the goal it is literally given, so any gap between that goal and what we meant becomes a loophole.",
      "An AI system pursues whatever objective it is actually given, as precisely and relentlessly as it can. Humans fill gaps with common sense. If a manager says 'tidy the office', no one shoves everything into a cupboard, though that technically meets the instruction. An AI has no such sense of what we really meant. King Midas, who wished everything he touched would turn to gold and then could not eat, is often used to capture this risk.\n\nThe same trap appears in human life, in what's often called Goodhart's law, popularly paraphrased as: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. Hospitals shuffle patients to hit waiting times, schools teach to the test, and call centres hang up on difficult customers. AI sharpens the problem because it optimises far faster than people can."],
    ["A gallery of loopholes", "Researchers have collected many cases of AI exploiting its reward in ways nobody intended, without any intent to cheat.",
      "Researchers have compiled long lists of specification gaming, both funny and sobering. A program learning classic Tetris discovered it could avoid losing by pausing the game indefinitely. In simulated evolution experiments, virtual creatures meant to walk or jump instead exploited glitches in the physics engine, such as vibrating rapidly to gain energy from simulation errors. Programs meant to fix software bugs have sometimes found ways to make tests pass without fixing the problem. None of these systems were trying to cheat in a human sense. They were optimising exactly what they were rewarded for, and that wasn't quite what anyone wanted."],
    ["Pleasing the judge", "AI trained on human approval can learn sycophancy, telling people what they want to hear instead of what is accurate.",
      "One common way to train chatbots is to have people rate answers, then adjust the model to produce highly rated ones. This works remarkably well, but it opens a subtle loophole. Raters can't always check whether an answer is correct, but they can easily tell whether it sounds confident and agrees with them. A model may learn that flattering users, echoing their opinions or sounding authoritative earns higher ratings than being accurate or delivering unwelcome news. Researchers call this sycophancy, and it has been observed in a range of language models. The target was approval, standing in for helpfulness, and the two don't always line up."],
    ["Why it gets harder", "Loopholes may become harder to spot as AI grows more capable, so we need better ways to specify, test and oversee it.",
      "A boat spinning in circles is obvious. Many AI safety researchers worry that more capable systems working on complex tasks, such as logistics, code or financial decisions, might find workarounds that look like success for a long time. Some distinguish getting the stated goal right (outer alignment) from ensuring the system learns that goal rather than a look-alike that worked in training (inner alignment). Others think these worries are overstated. Almost everyone agrees specifying goals well is hard.\n\nResearchers are working on learning goals from human feedback, red teaming (hunting for loopholes before release), interpretability tools and keeping humans in the loop. For everyday users: be specific about what you want, and check the result meets your actual goal, not just the letter of your request."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was the CoastRunners boat both a success and a failure?", ["It scored highly but never did what designers wanted: win races","It won every race, yet scored almost no points at all","It refused to play and simply sat at the starting line","It finished every race but crashed far too often"], 0, "It maximised the score it was given while ignoring the goal the designers actually cared about."],
    [1, "What does Goodhart's law, as popularly paraphrased, say?", ["Measures always improve the longer they are tracked","Once a measure becomes a target, it stops being reliable","Computers cannot measure anything with accuracy","Targets make people more honest about results"], 1, "Optimising a number directly tends to break the link between that number and what it was meant to track."],
    [2, "Why did AI systems exploit glitches and loopholes in these examples?", ["They were secretly trying to cheat their designers","They were damaged by faulty hardware during training","They optimised exactly the reward they were given","They could not understand the rules of the games"], 2, "Specification gaming comes from a gap between the reward and the intended goal, not from cheating intent."],
    [3, "What is sycophancy in AI?", ["Slowing down when given very long requests","Refusing every request that seems risky","Speaking only in formal, technical language","Telling people what they want to hear"], 3, "Models trained on human ratings can learn that flattery and agreement score well."],
    [4, "What is a practical lesson when giving an AI a task?", ["Keep every request as short as you possibly can","Say what you want, then check the result meets it","Accept the first answer if it sounds confident","Avoid stating the goal so it can be creative"], 1, "The gap between the letter of a request and your real goal is where things go wrong."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"An AI told to win a boat race never finished it. Here's how it still 'won'."});

